# Nx Streaming from Zarr

## Introduction

Two ways to turn a Zarr array into Nx tensors:

- `ExZarr.Nx.stream_chunk_tensors/2` — one tensor per stored chunk (`Array.stream_chunks/2`)
- `ExZarr.Nx.DataLoader` — fixed-size batches along the first axis (`Array.get_slice/2`)

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:ex_zarr, "~> 1.3"},
  # {:ex_zarr, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "../..")}
  {:nx, "~> 0.7"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.13"}
])
```

## Section

Create a small in-memory array. The last chunk is shorter than `chunks`.

```elixir
alias ExZarr.Array

{:ok, array} =
  Array.create(
    shape: {10, 4},
    chunks: {4, 4},
    dtype: :float32,
    storage: :memory
  )

data = for i <- 0..39, into: <<>>, do: <<i * 1.0::float-little-32>>
:ok = Array.set_slice(array, data, start: {0, 0}, stop: {10, 4})

:ok
```

## Section

`stream_chunk_tensors/2` yields `{:ok, tensor}`. Edge chunks are cropped to the array bounds. `:concurrency` and `:on_error` are forwarded to `stream_chunks/2`.

```elixir
chunk_shapes =
  array
  |> ExZarr.Nx.stream_chunk_tensors(concurrency: 2, ordered: true)
  |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, tensor} -> Nx.shape(tensor) end)

Kino.inspect(chunk_shapes)
```

## Section

Sum each stored chunk, then add the partial sums. This stays chunk-aligned and does not build a sample index.

```elixir
total =
  array
  |> ExZarr.Nx.stream_chunk_tensors()
  |> Enum.reduce(0.0, fn {:ok, tensor}, acc ->
    acc + (Nx.sum(tensor) |> Nx.to_number())
  end)

total
```

## Section

`to_tensor_chunked/3` ignores the stored grid and reads regions of the size you pass.

```elixir
region_shapes =
  array
  |> ExZarr.Nx.to_tensor_chunked({3, 4})
  |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, tensor} -> Nx.shape(tensor) end)

region_shapes
```

## Section

`DataLoader.batch_stream/3` slices along axis 0. A batch can cross chunk boundaries. `drop_remainder: true` drops a short final batch.

```elixir
batch_shapes =
  array
  |> ExZarr.Nx.DataLoader.batch_stream(3, drop_remainder: true)
  |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, batch} -> Nx.shape(batch) end)

batch_shapes
```

## Section

Shuffling permutes every sample index for the epoch. `:shuffle_buffer_size` is ignored. Pass `:seed` when you need the same order again.

```elixir
first_rows =
  array
  |> ExZarr.Nx.DataLoader.shuffled_batch_stream(2, seed: 7, drop_remainder: true)
  |> Enum.take(2)
  |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, batch} -> Nx.to_list(batch) end)

first_rows
```

## Section

Pair a feature array with a label array. Both must have the same length on axis 0.

```elixir
{:ok, labels} =
  Array.create(shape: {10}, chunks: {4}, dtype: :float32, storage: :memory)

label_data = for i <- 0..9, into: <<>>, do: <<(i * 10.0)::float-little-32>>
:ok = Array.set_slice(labels, label_data, start: {0}, stop: {10})

pairs =
  ExZarr.Nx.DataLoader.paired_batch_stream(array, labels, 4)
  |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, {x, y}} -> {Nx.shape(x), Nx.shape(y)} end)

pairs
```

## Key Takeaways

1. Use `stream_chunk_tensors/2` for chunk-aligned map/reduce and `:concurrency`.
2. Use `DataLoader.batch_stream/3` when the step needs a fixed sample count.
3. Use `paired_batch_stream/4` so features and labels stay aligned.
4. `shuffled_batch_stream/3` keeps the full index list in memory for the epoch.
